Upgrade procedure for libsodium

Source: https://download.libsodium.org/libsodium/releases/libsodium-VERSION.tar.gz
	Check libsodium-VERSION.tar.gz.minisig with minisign and Frank
	Denis's key RWQf6LRCGA9i53mlYecO4IzT51TGPpvWucNSCh1CBM0QTaLn73Y7GFO3
	(published on https://doc.libsodium.org/installation and in the
	minisign README).  Releases that are no longer supported move to
	releases/old/ and later releases/old/unsupported/.

The vendor branch (vendor/libsodium) carries the complete release
tarball.  Pruning happens only in sys/contrib/libsodium; see
MIDNIGHTBSD-Xlist (autotools output only).

History: sys/contrib/libsodium came from FreeBSD, which imported
upstream git commit 461ac93b260b (1.0.16-97-g461ac93b, August 2018)
rather than a release.  The vendor branch therefore has, after the
initial snapshot commit 3a5064fed8, a commit with that exact upstream
tree ("Vendor import of libsodium 1.0.16-97-g461ac93b") and then the
1.0.22 release tarball.

sys/contrib/libsodium is used only by the kernel: sys/conf/files and
sys/modules/crypto build a small set of C files from it (ChaCha20,
XChaCha20/HChaCha20, Poly1305 (donna), the ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEADs,
X25519 (ref10), ed25519_ref10 field arithmetic and crypto_verify).
The consumers are sys/crypto/chacha20_poly1305.c,
sys/crypto/curve25519.c (both used by wg(4)),
sys/opencrypto/xform_chacha20_poly1305.c and
sys/opencrypto/xform_poly1305.c.  The kernel glue in
sys/crypto/libsodium provides stub libc headers, randombytes_buf()
(arc4random_buf) and sodium_memzero()/sodium_is_zero().  No assembly
from libsodium is built, and of the configure HAVE_* macros only
HAVE_GCC_MEMORY_FENCES is defined (for the two AEAD files, so that
ACQUIRE_FENCE orders decryption after the tag check), so only the
portable reference code is compiled.  All libsodium files are built
with -DCONFIGURED=1, which private/common.h requires.

To upgrade to a new version:

1. Download and verify the tarball and extract it into a new directory.
2. Use the vendor-update skill (vendor/libsodium, sys/contrib/libsodium,
   tag vendor/libsodium/VERSION).  Stage with "git add -A -f" and check
   that the committed tree is identical to the tarball.
3. Merge.  Remove everything MIDNIGHTBSD-Xlist matches and extend the
   Xlist if needed.  Re-check the local patches listed below.
4. Check whether any file listed in sys/conf/files or
   sys/modules/crypto/Makefile moved, and whether the compiled files
   need new symbols (compare "nm -u" of crypto.ko before and after)
   or new headers in sys/crypto/libsodium.
5. Build the kernel and sys/modules/crypto, and build and run the
   upstream tests for the primitives above in userland (compile
   test/default/<name>.c against the new sources without the HAVE_*
   macros and compare with <name>.exp).

Local patches applied on top of upstream (from FreeBSD bbb7a2c7c329):

  - src/libsodium/include/sodium/core.h: the sodium_misuse()
    prototype is wrapped in "#ifndef _KERNEL".
  - src/libsodium/include/sodium/export.h, before the final #endif:
    under _KERNEL, include <sys/param.h> and <sys/libkern.h> and
    define sodium_misuse() as a static inline function that calls
    panic("bad value passed to sodium").
